1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,936
Every day, businesses move money across borders

2
00:00:02,936 --> 00:00:05,220
over rails built for a different era.

3
00:00:05,220 --> 00:00:06,354
It takes days.

4
00:00:06,354 --> 00:00:07,639
It costs a fortune.

5
00:00:07,639 --> 00:00:10,463
And everyone stares at the same word: pending.

6
00:00:10,463 --> 00:00:11,991
We think that problem is solved.

7
00:00:11,991 --> 00:00:13,414
This is Stellar Pay Hub.

8
00:00:14,364 --> 00:00:17,413
The average cross-border payment takes one to five

9
00:00:17,413 --> 00:00:20,582
business days, and eats two to seven percent in fees

10
00:00:20,582 --> 00:00:21,837
— before the spread.

11
00:00:21,837 --> 00:00:23,886
Merchants wait days to be paid.

12
00:00:23,886 --> 00:00:26,305
Payers cannot see where the money went.

13
00:00:26,305 --> 00:00:34,708
And developers inherit the rest: wallets, on-chain state,
reconciliation, webhooks, retries, audit logs.

14
00:00:34,708 --> 00:00:37,485
Stellar made settlement fast and cheap.

15
00:00:37,485 --> 00:00:38,944
It did not ship the product.

16
00:00:39,910 --> 00:00:41,214
So here is the fix.

17
00:00:41,214 --> 00:00:45,068
Stellar Pay Hub is one platform on Stellar and Soroban.

18
00:00:45,068 --> 00:00:46,790
Payers check out with a link or

19
00:00:46,790 --> 00:00:48,619
an invoice, and sign in their own

20
00:00:48,619 --> 00:00:49,050
wallet.

21
00:00:49,050 --> 00:00:54,073
Merchants get a real workspace: revenue, settlement,
reconciliation.

22
00:00:54,073 --> 00:01:01,098
And six smart contracts run escrow, invoicing, subscriptions,
treasury, and settlement on-chain.

23
00:01:01,098 --> 00:01:03,633
Everything you would normally bolt on afterwards?

24
00:01:03,633 --> 00:01:04,582
Already here.

25
00:01:05,528 --> 00:01:06,763
Let us start with the payer.

26
00:01:06,763 --> 00:01:09,448
A merchant shares a payment link or an invoice.

27
00:01:09,448 --> 00:01:12,307
The checkout shows the merchant, the amount,

28
00:01:12,307 --> 00:01:15,038
the asset, and the network — no surprises.

29
00:01:15,038 --> 00:01:18,522
The payer connects Freighter or xBull, and signs.

30
00:01:18,522 --> 00:01:19,737
No card numbers.

31
00:01:19,737 --> 00:01:21,242
No bank details.

32
00:01:21,242 --> 00:01:22,411
No redirects.

33
00:01:23,370 --> 00:01:25,894
Invoices work the same way — line items,

34
00:01:25,894 --> 00:01:28,295
amount, and due date straight from the

35
00:01:28,295 --> 00:01:28,910
merchant.

36
00:01:28,910 --> 00:01:32,629
Every intent is signed with the payer key, and carries

37
00:01:32,629 --> 00:01:36,077
an idempotency key, so a retried request can never

38
00:01:36,077 --> 00:01:37,024
charge twice.

39
00:01:37,972 --> 00:01:40,225
On the merchant side: the workspace.

40
00:01:40,225 --> 00:01:44,068
Products, customers, invoices, payment links, and settlements

41
00:01:44,068 --> 00:01:48,470
in one place — with the settlement asset and the on-chain
registration

42
00:01:48,470 --> 00:01:49,461
right up front.

43
00:01:50,414 --> 00:01:53,852
The transaction history is the operational view:

44
00:01:53,852 --> 00:01:57,922
amount, asset, direction, counterparty, hash, and status.

45
00:01:57,922 --> 00:02:00,243
And a row only reads confirmed when

46
00:02:00,243 --> 00:02:03,015
the chain says so — never on an optimistic

47
00:02:03,015 --> 00:02:03,466
write.

48
00:02:04,416 --> 00:02:06,472
Operators get a console of their own.

49
00:02:06,472 --> 00:02:07,815
Platform analytics.

50
00:02:07,815 --> 00:02:09,343
Transaction monitoring.

51
00:02:09,343 --> 00:02:11,334
Merchant and user management.

52
00:02:11,334 --> 00:02:12,954
The asset registry.

53
00:02:12,954 --> 00:02:14,806
Notification broadcasts.

54
00:02:14,806 --> 00:02:17,838
And an audit log written by the API itself.

55
00:02:17,838 --> 00:02:20,161
That is the layer payment products usually

56
00:02:20,161 --> 00:02:22,213
grow into painfully — here, it ships.

57
00:02:23,170 --> 00:02:26,974
Underneath, six Soroban contracts are live on testnet.

58
00:02:26,974 --> 00:02:29,293
Escrow locks funds, and releases them on

59
00:02:29,293 --> 00:02:31,500
a schedule — with an optional arbiter.

60
00:02:31,500 --> 00:02:34,034
Invoices are issued and paid on-chain.

61
00:02:34,034 --> 00:02:39,150
Subscriptions bill by interval, executed by the contract — not
a cron job.

62
00:02:39,150 --> 00:02:44,914
Treasury runs withdrawals as threshold approvals — governance,
not one admin key.

63
00:02:44,914 --> 00:02:48,363
And settlement closes a period, and pays out.

64
00:02:49,316 --> 00:02:52,472
And here is the escrow contract itself, in Rust.

65
00:02:52,472 --> 00:02:55,782
Funds are held by the contract — never by an admin wallet.

66
00:02:55,782 --> 00:02:58,617
Release needs the payer, the payee, or an appointed

67
00:02:58,617 --> 00:03:01,178
arbiter, and the ledger clock must have passed

68
00:03:01,178 --> 00:03:02,160
the release time.

69
00:03:02,160 --> 00:03:04,602
Every state change publishes an event.

70
00:03:04,602 --> 00:03:07,611
That event is exactly what the indexer consumes.

71
00:03:08,574 --> 00:03:10,876
That is the decision behind the whole

72
00:03:10,876 --> 00:03:13,420
platform: the API never invents a status.

73
00:03:13,420 --> 00:03:18,147
A dedicated indexer watches Horizon and Soroban RPC,
deduplicates

74
00:03:18,147 --> 00:03:23,303
every event, and only then advances an escrow to funded, a
subscription

75
00:03:23,303 --> 00:03:24,091
to active.

76
00:03:24,091 --> 00:03:26,013
If the chain did not say it happened,

77
00:03:26,013 --> 00:03:27,783
the product does not claim it did.

78
00:03:28,744 --> 00:03:34,075
The architecture is a typed monorepo, end to end: a NestJS API
on Postgres and

79
00:03:34,075 --> 00:03:39,542
Prisma, Redis for sessions and rate limits, Socket.IO for
realtime, and an event

80
00:03:39,542 --> 00:03:41,634
indexer in front of the chain.

81
00:03:41,634 --> 00:03:44,399
One typed SDK is the single client contract —

82
00:03:44,399 --> 00:03:47,163
shared by the web app, the admin console, and

83
00:03:47,163 --> 00:03:48,486
the Chrome extension.

84
00:03:48,486 --> 00:03:51,322
And one Zod schema validates the API and its

85
00:03:51,322 --> 00:03:54,537
clients alike, so a shape change breaks the build,

86
00:03:54,537 --> 00:03:55,546
not production.

87
00:03:56,498 --> 00:03:58,485
Security is first class.

88
00:03:58,485 --> 00:04:03,913
Authentication is wallet-native, with Ed25519 challenges.

89
00:04:03,913 --> 00:04:06,528
Sessions can be revoked — and realtime

90
00:04:06,528 --> 00:04:10,082
sockets re-check them, so revocation actually bites.

91
00:04:10,082 --> 00:04:14,457
Webhook delivery refuses private network targets, and applies
a timeout.

92
00:04:14,457 --> 00:04:16,853
And the audit log records what happened,

93
00:04:16,853 --> 00:04:19,365
without ever writing a credential into it.

94
00:04:20,316 --> 00:04:22,974
And it is verified — not slideware.

95
00:04:22,974 --> 00:04:26,007
Four hundred forty six unit and integration tests.

96
00:04:26,007 --> 00:04:28,785
Seventy six across the six contracts.

97
00:04:28,785 --> 00:04:34,280
CI runs type checks, lint, the full suite, and a Postgres and
Redis integration spec — plus two

98
00:04:34,280 --> 00:04:39,947
live testnet suites that drive a real payment from challenge
to on-chain confirmation, then assert

99
00:04:39,947 --> 00:04:42,523
the persisted state and the realtime update.

100
00:04:43,486 --> 00:04:44,628
So what does that buy you?

101
00:04:44,628 --> 00:04:47,846
Settlement in about five seconds, not days.

102
00:04:47,846 --> 00:04:50,739
Fees at a fraction of a cent, not percentages.

103
00:04:50,739 --> 00:04:56,538
Escrow, subscriptions, and treasury as product features — not
three separate vendors.

104
00:04:56,538 --> 00:05:03,459
Plus a working reference implementation: auth, indexer,
reconciliation, admin console, tests.

105
00:05:03,459 --> 00:05:06,480
Against a card processor, you own the money movement.

106
00:05:06,480 --> 00:05:10,473
Against a wallet and a hope, you get the whole operational
layer.

107
00:05:11,432 --> 00:05:13,751
Stellar Pay Hub: the rails, the contracts,

108
00:05:13,751 --> 00:05:16,178
and the product — already built, and already

109
00:05:16,178 --> 00:05:16,718
deployed.

110
00:05:16,718 --> 00:05:19,119
The frontends are live on Vercel, and

111
00:05:19,119 --> 00:05:21,519
the six contracts are live on Stellar

112
00:05:21,519 --> 00:05:22,088
testnet.

113
00:05:22,088 --> 00:05:24,271
Clone it, bring the stack up locally,

114
00:05:24,271 --> 00:05:26,282
and watch a payment settle in five

115
00:05:26,282 --> 00:05:26,799
seconds.
